"split.help.intro" = "Splitting cuts one big file into numbered pieces, and joining puts them back together. Useful when a file will not fit on a stick, through a mail attachment or onto a disc.";
"split.help.how.title" = "How to split";
"split.help.how.body" = "Put the cursor on the file and open Tools ▸ Split file. Choose a part size — the buttons offer the usual ones — and where the pieces go. By default they land in the OTHER panel's folder, so point that side at the destination first.";
"split.help.names.title" = "What the parts are called";
"split.help.names.body" = "archive.zip becomes archive.zip.001, archive.zip.002 and so on. That is the same naming Total Commander and HJSplit use, so the pieces can be rejoined by those tools too.";
"split.help.join.title" = "How to join them back";
"split.help.join.body" = "Put the cursor on ANY of the parts and open Tools ▸ Join parts — the rest are found and ordered automatically. The window shows how many were found and where the result will go, and offers to move the parts to the Trash once it is done. That only happens when the checksum confirms the result is intact; if it does not match, the parts stay, because at that moment they are the only complete copy.";
"split.help.checksum.title" = "The checksum file";
"split.help.checksum.body" = "With that option on, splitting also writes a small .sha256 file. When you join, it is used to check the result: a set with a missing or damaged part still produces a plausible-looking file, and only the checksum catches it. You are told either “the checksum matches” or that a part is missing.";
"join.partsFound" = "Parts found";
