/* Help: Git state */
"git.help.intro" = "Git keeps the history of a folder: you work in it, and it remembers what everything looked like at the last save. Such a folder is called a repository. The panel shows what the history already knows about and what you have changed since — the same facts as “git status”, without the terminal and for the whole folder at once.";
"git.help.letters.title" = "The letters on files";
"git.help.letters.body" = "One coloured letter stands before the name:\n\nM (orange) — changed since the last save.\nA (green) — staged, i.e. set aside for the next save.\nD (red) — deleted.\nR (blue) — renamed.\n? (teal) — new; the history knows nothing about it yet.\n! (red) — a merge conflict: two changes claim the same place, and only a person can settle it.\n· (faint dot) — ignored on purpose (.gitignore): build output, caches, temporary files.\n\nAn unchanged file carries no mark — that IS “nothing to save”. Hover a letter and it explains itself in words.";
"git.help.folders.title" = "The mark on a folder";
"git.help.folders.body" = "A folder wears the strongest mark of everything inside it, however deep. A change in app/Views/Panel puts an M on app, so the letters can be followed down to it instead of opening folders at random. The order of importance: conflict beats deletion, deletion beats change, change beats a new file, a new file beats an ignored one.";
"git.help.branch.title" = "The branch and the dot on a repository folder";
"git.help.branch.body" = "When the folder itself is a repository, its branch is written at the trailing end of the row with a ⑂ mark. A branch is a line of work of its own — usually “main” for the settled state and a branch per new thing. The orange dot beside it means uncommitted work: changes the history does not know about yet. No dot, nothing pending.\n\nIn a folder full of projects this is the useful part: who is on which branch, and where work is left unsaved.";
"git.help.where.title" = "Where it shows";
"git.help.where.body" = "In all three list modes. Detailed and brief put the letters in a column right after the icon and the branch at the trailing edge; thumbnails lay a small plaque on the picture itself, bottom centre, so it reads over any background. On the cursor row the mark turns white — a state colour can land close to the cursor bar and disappear into it.";
"git.help.source.title" = "Where the facts come from";
"git.help.source.body" = "The panel asks the git installed on the machine — the same one you use in the terminal. That is deliberate: a repository is not a file format but a set of rules (ignore files, the index, submodules, worktrees), and the only reader that is right about all of them is git itself.\n\nThe question is asked in the background; the panel never waits on it. The folder's own letters come first, the branch names right after (those are plain file reads, no process), and the “uncommitted” dot last: it costs one query per repository, so they run four at a time, and past forty repositories only the branch name is shown. The last twelve folders are remembered, so coming back shows the marks at once.\n\nThe program only reads. Nothing is saved, sent or changed in your history.";
"git.help.off.title" = "If it is in the way";
"git.help.off.body" = "Settings ▸ List ▸ “Show Git marks in the list”. It takes effect at once, no restart. Outside a repository the list looks exactly as it did without the feature: the column takes no width when there is nothing to show. Archives, remote connections, the Trash and the shelf are never asked — there is no working tree there to ask about.";

