/* Help: folder rules */
"rules.help.button" = "How this works";
"rules.help.intro" = "A rule is a habit written down once: “a file looks like THIS — do THAT with it”. You describe it here, and it runs on your command — Tools ▸ Apply Rules to This Folder — over the folder open in the active panel. Left alone, rules do nothing.";
"rules.help.order.title" = "The order of the rules is the meaning";
"rules.help.order.body" = "Rules are read top to bottom and a file is taken by the FIRST one that matches; the rest never see it. Two rules wanting the same file would otherwise pull it in different directions, and the second would act on a plan already out of date. Rearrange with the ⌃⌄ arrows under the list. The switch by a name turns a rule off without deleting it.";
"rules.help.head.title" = "Name, “Runs”, “Act on folders too”";
"rules.help.head.body" = "The name is for you — it appears in the apply list so you can see which rule took a file.\n\n“Runs when everything matches” means the file must pass every condition; “when anything matches” means one is enough.\n\n“Act on folders too” is off by default: a rule written for downloads should not also carry away the folder you just unpacked an archive into.";
"rules.help.conditions.title" = "Conditions: what can be asked about a file";
"rules.help.conditions.body" = "Name — compared against a mask.\nExtension — a comma-separated list: “jpg, png, heic”. The leading dot is optional, case does not matter.\nKind — the family rather than the format: image, video, audio, pdf, text, archive, folder.\nSize — in megabytes.\nModified, Created, Added — age in days by that date. If the filesystem gave no such date the condition answers no rather than “very old” — otherwise a dateless file would be swept into the Trash.\nTag — Finder's colour tags by name: Red, Green, Blue…";
"rules.help.tests.title" = "Tests";
"rules.help.tests.body" = "Each field offers only what suits it: a name gets “matches” and “does not match”, a list gets “is one of” and “is not one of”, a size gets “bigger” and “smaller”, a date gets “older” and “newer”. An empty mask does not mean “everything” — an unfinished rule takes no files at all.";
"rules.help.actions.title" = "Actions";
"rules.help.actions.body" = "Move and Copy — into the chosen folder.\nRename — by masks, the same language as the group rename.\nMove to Trash — into the Trash, never past it.\nAdd tag — a Finder colour tag; the file stays where it is.\nPut on the shelf — also leaves the file in place.\nUnpack — the archive is unpacked beside itself, into a folder of its own name.";
"rules.help.destination.title" = "“Where to” and “Subfolder”";
"rules.help.destination.body" = "“Where to” is the destination folder — type a path or pick one.\n\n“Subfolder” sorts files inside it by date: “yyyy” gives 2026, “yyyy/MM” gives 2026/08, “yyyy-MM” gives 2026-08. It uses the FILE's own date, not today's, so last year's photographs land in last year's folder. Missing folders are created. Leave it empty to drop everything straight into the destination.";
"rules.help.masks.title" = "Name masks";
"rules.help.masks.body" = "In the Name condition the mask is the panel's own: “*” for any part of a name, “?” for one character, a space separates several patterns — “счёт*”, “*report*”, “IMG_*”.\n\nIn the Rename action the masks are the group-rename language instead: [N] the name, [E] the extension, [C] a counter, [Y][M][D] year, month, day. “invoice-[N]” turns “2026.pdf” into “invoice-2026.pdf”.";
"rules.help.apply.title" = "Running them";
"rules.help.apply.body" = "Stand in the folder and choose Tools ▸ Apply Rules to This Folder. A list opens first: which file, which rule took it, what will happen and where it lands. Every line has a switch; below is a count, and separately how many files are going to the Trash. Only what stays ticked happens.";
"rules.help.safety.title" = "What cannot happen";
"rules.help.safety.body" = "Nothing happens silently: without your “Apply” not one file moves. Every action goes down the road it already has in this program — the same copier with its own name-clash questions, the same Trash, the same unpacker — so moves and deletions undo with Cmd+Z. There is no permanent deletion among the actions at all. Rules run only in ordinary folders on disk: not in an archive, not on a remote connection, not in the Trash or on the shelf.";

